 &nbsp; 
 
 Emptying out the notebook before meal prep... 

 😱 Look out below: Anthropic's IPO pop has turned into a thud, leaving retail investors holding the bag.

 Shares closed yesterday at $81.90 — below the $83 IPO price and 26.5% below the first post-IPO trades. They opened this morning down another 5% at $77.93 per share. Same story at SpaceX, where shares today opened below their IPO price, 25% below the first post-IPO trades, and around 44% below the first-day high. Yes, these are just moments in time and shouldn't worry long-term investors in either company. But they could be causing some sleepless nights over at OpenAI, assuming it still plans to go public in early 2027. Big fund managers might push much harder on price than they did with either Anthropic or SpaceX, and a lot of retail investors might sit it out entirely — not wanting more wounds to lick. ⛳ Next shot: BC Partners Credit this morning announced that it's acquired a majority stake in GSE Worldwide, a sports management agency known for representing pro golfers like Bryson DeChambeau. 

 BC already held a minority stake, but bought the rest from Gatemore Capital Management. This comes on the heels of BC bailing on its plans for LIV 2.0, and reports that it was working on a DIP plan that could have created yet another version. But a source assures me that "LIV 3.0 is dead" and that GSE is a standalone investment. 🏛️ What to watch: Rep. Jamie Raskin (D-Md.), incoming chair of the House Judiciary Committee, says that he plans to pursue his investigation of 1789 Capital — the growth equity firm that Donald Trump Jr. joined just days after his father's election victory in 2024.

 It's something I discussed with Trump Jr. and 1789 founder Omeed Malik last week at Axios BFD , given that the firm had previously declined certain information requests from Raskin. But Raskin might want a private equity primer before holding hearings. On CNN last night , he said the firm was "worth" $3 billion when he meant its AUM. He also said "their stock has shot up," even though 1789 doesn't have a public stock. 📸 Speaking of Axios BFD: Some of you emailed to ask what happened to our planned interview with Flock CEO Garrett Langley. 

 In short, Langley reached out in late October to say a personal conflict had arisen and he could no longer attend. At the same time, however, Langley turned down a different Axios request at a different date and said at a private event that he'd prefer to never do media interviews again. So who really knows. It's a shame, because Flock has raised over $1 billion and really does have a compelling story to tell. Keeping quiet is unlikely to make the storm dissipate. 💰 Corporate venturing: Payward, owner of crypto exchange Kraken, earlier this week announced that it had acquired Tribe Capital.

 Tribe was co-founded by Payward co-CEO Arjun Sethi, years before he joined Payward, and has raised over $2 billion for various VC funds. Sethi tells me that the firm will continue to raise money from outside LPs, but now will have "better access" to equity and debt products for its portfolio. Plus expanded distribution. Don't be surprised to see Payward buy other managers, while also augmenting the Tribe team. Also: Sethi didn't respond to my questions on Foundation, a Tribe portfolio company that stopped paying its employees last month. This is the one led by Sankaet Pathak, who founded Foundation while customers of his prior company (Synapse) were still unable to access around $50 million in deposits.

 Oh, Pathak also borrowed $4.6 million from Foundation last year to buy a luxury home. At the time of the Tribe investment, we wrote : "Silicon Valley has a history of giving founders a second chance, but usually it waits until the initial fire has been extinguished." 
 
