 Final Numbers 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: Bureau of Economic Analysis ; Chart: Courtenay Brown/Axios The U.S. economy grew substantially faster this summer than initially thought, per new data released this morning.

 GDP expanded at a 4.3% annualized rate , up from the 3.4% initially reported and nearly twice the rate of 2.2% in the second quarter. Other new economic data: Personal income jumped 0.7% in October while inflation-adjusted consumer spending stalled, pushing the saving rate up to 4.1% from 3.6% the prior month.

 The PCE price index— the Fed's preferred inflation gauge — rose 3.8% from a year earlier, up from 3.6% in September. Core inflation, which excludes food and energy, accelerated to 3.3% from 3.1%. 
 
